--- body ---
## Observation

The after-merge runner's record for main tip 0645f247 (PR #2023, result
fail, written 2026-10-29T23:37:24Z) never reached origin's refs/notes/gate,
although the runner printed it, which it does only after `append_and_push`
returns, and went on to open red ticket 1364 (PR #2025). The local reflog
of refs/notes/gate shows:

- 00:38:07 +0100 `notes append` -> 9a85f48c (the 0645f247 record, parent ac5b37cb)
- 00:38:08 +0100 `fetch -q origin +refs/notes/gate:refs/notes/gate: forced-update` -> ac5b37cb
- 00:38:21 next append on ac5b37cb, pushed; origin's history never holds 9a85f48c

No "notes push rejected" warning was logged for that run. No writer
force-pushes refs/notes/gate (merge-gate.py and review_notes.py both push
without `+`). The forced fetch is `fetch_notes`, which merge-gate.py runs
before every append, check and record, from any process sharing this
repository's refs (raid_land for #2025 was starting at that moment).

Cause not yet established. Candidates: the push returned 0 but the forge
served the pre-push ref to a fetch one second later (read-after-write lag),
and the next writer's non-forced push still landed on top of the older tip;
or the push did not land and its failure was not reported.

`main_sweep.py --after` reported the tip as "the runner crashed on it",
which was wrong: it ran and recorded red.

Repaired 2026-10-30 by appending the reflog's blob verbatim to origin's
refs/notes/gate (read back; the after-merge audit reads clean).

## Exit criteria

- One reproduction or a decisive discriminator between the two candidates
  (for example `git ls-remote origin refs/notes/gate` read back right after
  the push in `append_and_push`).
- `append_and_push` confirms its record on the remote before returning, or
  the ticket records why that is not needed.

## Scope note (raid 85542d)

`main_sweep.py --after` labels an attempted tip without a remote record
"the runner crashed on them". That label is wrong when the runner wrote a
record that was lost before reaching the remote; the wording changes to
name both outcomes.

## Imagine (raid 85542d)

A third candidate fits every observation better than the two above: a local
race on the shared ref. `append_and_push` appends (local refs/notes/gate ->
9a85f48c), another process's `fetch_notes` force-fetches the same ref in the
same repository (-> ac5b37cb, the 00:38:08 reflog line), then the push names
the ref, `refs/notes/gate:refs/notes/gate`, finds nothing new to send, and
exits 0 with no warning. Reproduced deterministically with a bare remote
(append, forced fetch, push by name: exit 0, remote at the old tip; push by
OID lands). Forge lag alone does not fit: the next writer's non-forced push
at 00:38:21 succeeded with no rejection, so origin did not hold 9a85f48c then.

## Plan

1. `append_and_push` captures the OID of the notes ref right after its
   append and pushes `<oid>:refs/notes/gate`, so a concurrent forced fetch
   cannot change what is pushed.
2. Read-back (the discriminator going forward): after a push that exits 0,
   `git ls-remote <remote> refs/notes/gate`; confirmed when the remote tip is
   the OID, or descends from it (another writer appended after: check
   ancestry after a fetch). Otherwise log a distinct warning naming pushed
   and remote OIDs ("push reported success but the remote lacks the
   record"), re-check briefly to tell forge lag from loss, then retry inside
   the existing attempt loop. A retry must not leave a duplicate entry that
   changes any reader's result.
3. `main_sweep.py`: reword the "crashed" label.
4. First test (tests/test_merge_gate.py, bare-remote fixture): simulate the
   rewind between append and push (wrap the module's `git` so a forced fetch
   of the ref runs just before the push) and assert the record is on the
   remote; it must fail on the pre-fix code. Second test: a read-back
   mismatch warns and retries; a third: an ls-remote failure on read-back
   does not silently pass.

## Feasibility (raid 85542d)

PASS. Callers: merge-gate.py record (~609) and run-after (~1026); no other
writer of refs/notes/gate. review_notes.py serializes under a lock and merges
rather than force-fetching: out of scope.

## Blind-spot pass (raid 85542d), folded into the plan

- Capture race: another process's forced fetch can also land between the
  append and the OID capture. Build the notes commit on a per-process ref
  (or verify the captured commit holds the entry) so the pushed OID is
  always the one carrying this record.
- Read-back fetches the remote tip into a probe ref, never with `+` onto
  refs/notes/gate. `ls-remote` exit 2 (ref absent) is loss; any other
  non-zero is "cannot verify", a distinct warning, not loss. Forge lag ends
  in a warning, never a GateError, unless the record is provably absent
  after a refetch.
- Before re-appending on a retry, check whether the record already reached
  the remote (ancestry); decide() and after_entries take the last entry, so
  a duplicate is harmless but avoidable.
- Remotes may be a local path (raid_land without origin); keep
  test_merge_gate.py, test_after_merge.py and test_raid_land_local.py green.
- main_sweep: the gap label also fires when the local ref was rewound and
  the record is on the remote; use neutral wording ("attempted, no record
  visible").
- Check whether hooks/reference-transaction reads the local gate ref; record
  the answer in the PR.
